body:
## What it does
JSON batching sends up to 20 individual requests in a single HTTP call. It reduces round trips for dashboards and sync jobs, and it also moves very long $filter strings out of the URL into the body. Each inner request is still evaluated on its own for permissions and throttling.

## Request
```http
POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/$batch
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer [access-token]

{
  "requests": [
    {
      "id": "1",
      "method": "GET",
      "url": "/me?$select=displayName,userPrincipalName"
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "method": "GET",
      "url": "/me/messages?$filter=isRead eq false&$select=subject,from,receivedDateTime&$top=5"
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "method": "GET",
      "url": "/users?$filter=startsWith(displayName,'A')&$orderby=displayName&$count=true&$top=10&$select=displayName",
      "headers": { "ConsistencyLevel": "eventual" }
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "method": "GET",
      "url": "/me/memberOf/microsoft.graph.group?$select=id,displayName",
      "headers": { "ConsistencyLevel": "eventual" }
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "method": "GET",
      "url": "/me/drive/recent?$top=5"
    }
  ]
}
```

Rules visible in the example: url is relative and starts with a slash, without version or host. Each request needs a unique id. A request with a body must carry a Content-Type header. An OData type cast such as /memberOf/microsoft.graph.group needs ConsistencyLevel: eventual, set in the inner headers. This first batch is fully parallel: no request has dependsOn.

## Response
```json
{
  "responses": [
    { "id": "1", "status": 200, "headers": { "Content-Type": "application/json" }, "body": { "displayName": "Anna Meyer" } },
    { "id": "2", "status": 200, "body": { "value": [] } },
    { "id": "3", "status": 200, "body": { "@odata.count": 3, "value": [] } },
    { "id": "4", "status": 403, "body": { "error": { "code": "Authorization_RequestDenied" } } },
    { "id": "5", "status": 200, "body": { "value": [] } }
  ]
}
```
Responses can arrive in any order; match them by id. The outer status 200 only means the batch was parseable; check every inner status. Request 4 failed with 403 in this example, and requests 1, 2, 3 and 5 were not affected.

## Dependent requests (fully sequential batch)
Use dependsOn when one request must run after another, for example read after write. The documentation recommends that a batch is either fully sequential or fully parallel, so every request after the first lists its predecessor:
```http
POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/$batch
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "requests": [
    {
      "id": "1",
      "method": "PATCH",
      "url": "/users/[user-object-id]",
      "headers": { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      "body": { "department": "Sales" }
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "dependsOn": ["1"],
      "method": "GET",
      "url": "/users/[user-object-id]?$select=displayName,department"
    }
  ]
}
```
If request 1 fails, request 2 is not executed and returns status 424 (Failed Dependency). The batch cannot pass data from one request to the next; dependsOn only controls the order.

## A write example
```http
POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/$batch
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "requests": [
    {
      "id": "a",
      "method": "PATCH",
      "url": "/users/[user-object-id]",
      "headers": { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      "body": { "department": "Sales" }
    },
    {
      "id": "b",
      "method": "PATCH",
      "url": "/users/[other-user-object-id]",
      "headers": { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      "body": { "department": "Sales" }
    }
  ]
}
```

## How to adapt
- Bulk reads by id: GET /users/[id1], /users/[id2] and so on, 20 per call, to resolve many ids quickly.
- Combine requests for different workloads, but remember that the token must carry the permissions of all inner requests.
- Keep a batch fully parallel or fully sequential: the documentation recommends not mixing independent requests with dependsOn chains in one batch.

## Required permissions
There is no permission for the batch endpoint itself. Each inner request requires the permissions of its own resource, for example User.Read for /me and for /me/memberOf (GroupMember.Read.All or Directory.Read.All for broader group access), Mail.Read for /me/messages, Files.Read for /me/drive/recent. A missing permission only fails the affected inner request with 403.

## Common mistakes
- More than 20 requests in one batch, or a duplicate id, which fails the whole batch with 400.
- Absolute URLs or including /v1.0 in the inner url.
- Forgetting Content-Type on inner requests that have a body.
- Treating batch HTTP 200 as success for everything.
- Missing ConsistencyLevel for advanced queries inside the batch; set it in the inner headers.

## Performance and throttling notes
Batching reduces network overhead but not server-side limits: each inner request counts against throttling, and the underlying service (Exchange, SharePoint, directory) applies its own limits. Inner requests that exceed limits return 429 with a Retry-After header in the inner response headers; resend only those requests after the delay, not the whole batch.
